Site File Action <code>_default_user_can</code> <code>utils/permissioning.py</code> remove <code>if is_superuser: return True</code> (global fallback for Corpus/Document/etc.) <code>NoteManager.user_can</code> <code>shared/Managers.py</code> remove superuser bypass <code>AnnotationManager.user_can</code> <code>shared/Managers.py</code> remove blanket bypass; in the structural-write-deny branch, return <code>user.is_superuser</code> (superuser may write structural; others denied) so a non-structural superuser request is computed normally <code>RelationshipManager.user_can</code> <code>shared/Managers.py</code> same structural-exception pattern as annotations <p>Structural exception shape (annotations &amp; relationships):</p> <pre><code># structural items are read-only — except superusers may repair them
if instance.structural and perm in _WRITE_PERMS:
    return bool(getattr(user, "is_superuser", False))
# ... fall through to the normal (no-bypass) doc+corpus computation
</code></pre> <p>This keeps the existing invariant tests (<code>test_structural_*_are_read_only_for_non_superuser</code>, which assert the superuser can UPDATE a structural item) green, while a superuser's READ and non-structural authorization now match a normal user.</p>
